<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    GonogoKerbalismUplink's OWN contract slice: the fifteen wire payload types
    that used to live in mod/Sitrep.Contract/KerbalismPayloads.cs, relocated
    here per the operator mandate that NO uplink-specific wire type may live in
    core, even for an in-monorepo Uplink (see
    local_docs/design/2026-08-10-uplink-types-out-of-core-plan.md). Fifth Uplink
    through the types-out-of-core mechanism, and the largest by every measure:
    fifteen types against the previous high of five, FIVE [SitrepTopic]-tagged
    roots (kerbalism.spaceweather / .profile / .lifesupport / .crew /
    .features) against the previous high of two, and nesting three levels deep
    rather than two.

    The nesting is what makes this slice's generated shape map load-bearing
    rather than incidental: KerbalismSpaceWeather holds
    List<KerbalismStarInfo> and List<KerbalismStormEntry>,
    KerbalismCrewEntry holds List<KerbalismCrewRule>, KerbalismLifeSupport
    holds a KerbalismHabitat plus List<KerbalismProcessEntry> and
    List<KerbalismGreenhouseEntry>, and KerbalismProfile holds
    Dictionary<string, KerbalismResourceDef> plus two more lists. It is also
    the first slice where a Vec3 sits on a NESTED type
    (KerbalismStarInfo.Direction), so the unit has to travel down two levels of
    shape resolution and then out to the vector's three leaves.

    Deliberately its own small project rather than folding into
    GonogoKerbalismUplink.csproj, and the reason is sharper here than for any
    predecessor. GonogoKerbalismUplink.csproj is net48-only and carries
    UnityEngine/Assembly-CSharp HintPath references; rtcli cannot load that. It
    also could not host the netstandard2.0 codegen leg without dragging those
    references along. None of the fifteen types here touches Kerbalism or KSP
    at all (Kerbalism is reached exclusively by runtime reflection, see
    KerbalismReflection.cs), so the split costs nothing and keeps the codegen
    input clean. This project's dual-target boilerplate stays a straight copy
    of Sitrep.Contract.csproj's.

    MIT, matching GonogoKerbalismUplink.csproj. That project's own header
    records why the runtime half is MIT despite Kerbalism being Unlicense: it
    NEVER links Kerbalism's assembly and never derives from its types. This
    slice links even less (nothing but Sitrep.Contract), so it inherits the
    same licence with no new consideration. Kerbalism's attribution stays in
    ../GonogoKerbalismUplink/NOTICE-Kerbalism.txt.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net48</TargetFrameworks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <AssemblyName>GonogoKerbalismUplink.Contract</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>GonogoKerbalismUplink</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Reinforced.Typings is a compile-time-only codegen dependency: rtcli
       reads the [TsInterface] attributes baked into this assembly's metadata
       to generate this Uplink's own TS SDK slice (see codegen.sh). Must NOT
       flow to net48 (the runtime TFM GonogoKerbalismUplink.csproj bundles):
       same PrivateAssets/Condition/#if NETSTANDARD2_0 discipline as
       Sitrep.Contract.csproj, for the identical reason (a KSP-loaded net48
       assembly must never try to resolve Reinforced.Typings.dll). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Reinforced.Typings" Version="1.6.7" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'" PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="compile;build;native;contentfiles;analyzers;buildtransitive" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Sitrep.Contract only, Private="false": SitrepContractAttribute/
       SitrepTopicAttribute/SitrepUnitAttribute/Units are core's cross-package
       surface, provided by the core mod (GonogoCore) at runtime, never copied
       here. Vec3 comes from the same place and for the same reason: it is the
       ONE canonical vector shape every Domain's vector fields reuse (see its
       own doc comment on why the unit sits on the FIELD, not the type), so a
       per-Uplink copy would defeat the point. Same pattern
       GonogoKerbalismUplink.csproj already uses for its own Sitrep.Contract
       reference. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Sitrep.Contract\Sitrep.Contract.csproj">
      <Private>false</Private>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
